 ## Proof
 
-1.1 Let $\rho_A=\operatorname{Tr}_{BC}\rho_{ABC}$ and $\rho_{BC}=\operatorname{Tr}_A\rho_{ABC}$; [F9] makes them states. The operator $\rho_A\otimes\rho_{BC}$ is also a state: spectral decompositions of the two positive factors give nonnegative product eigenvalues, and their sum is $\operatorname{tr}(\rho_A)\operatorname{tr}(\rho_{BC})=1$. Applying [F2] to the bipartite states $\rho_{A|BC}$ and $\rho_{AB}$ gives support inclusion for both relative entropies $D(\rho_{ABC}\|\rho_A\otimes\rho_{BC})$ and $D(\rho_{AB}\|\rho_A\otimes\rho_B)$; [F1] then makes both finite. [given, F1, F2, F5, F7, F8, F9, algebra]
+1.1 View $\rho_{ABC}$ first across the grouped bipartition $A|BC$: [F9] makes $\rho_A=\operatorname{Tr}_{BC}\rho_{ABC}$ and $\rho_{BC}=\operatorname{Tr}_A\rho_{ABC}$ states. Applying [F9] across $AB|C$ also makes $\rho_{AB}=\operatorname{Tr}_C\rho_{ABC}$ a state. Applying it once more to $\rho_{AB}$ across $A|B$ makes $\rho_B=\operatorname{Tr}_A\rho_{AB}$ a state. This is also $\operatorname{Tr}_C\rho_{BC}$: in the ordered product basis [F6]–[F7], both have coefficient $\sum_{a,c}(\rho_{ABC})_{(a,b,c),(a,b^\prime,c)}$, since the finite sums may be reordered. The operator $\rho_A\otimes\rho_{BC}$ is also a state: spectral decompositions of the two positive factors give nonnegative product eigenvalues, and their sum is $\operatorname{tr}(\rho_A)\operatorname{tr}(\rho_{BC})=1$. Applying [F2] to the bipartite states $\rho_{A|BC}$ and $\rho_{AB}$ gives support inclusion for both relative entropies $D(\rho_{ABC}\|\rho_A\otimes\rho_{BC})$ and $D(\rho_{AB}\|\rho_A\otimes\rho_B)$; [F1] then makes both finite. [given, F1, F2, F5, F7, F8, F9, algebra]
 
 2.1 Set $X=\rho_{ABC}$ and $Y=\rho_A\otimes\rho_{BC}$. By [F6] and the ordered product basis [F7], $\operatorname{Tr}_C X=\rho_{AB}$ and $\operatorname{Tr}_C Y=\rho_A\otimes\operatorname{Tr}_C\rho_{BC}=\rho_A\otimes\rho_B$: the latter equality follows entrywise by summing the C-diagonal block of $\rho_{BC}$ while the A-factor matrix coefficient remains fixed. Apply [F4] to this pair to obtain $$D(\rho_{AB}\|\rho_A\otimes\rho_B)\le D(\rho_{ABC}\|\rho_A\otimes\rho_{BC}).$$ [given, F4, F6, F7, step 1.1, algebra]
 
